course: books
lesson: 26
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter I, part 2: The River Bank (the picnic basket, the Wild Wood and the Wide World)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Before they set off, the Rat climbs up into his hole and comes back staggering under a fat wicker picnic basket. When the Mole asks what's inside, the Rat rattles off a huge list of food all in one breath, and the Mole begs him to stop — it's too wonderful.
explain: Drifting along, the Mole trails a paw in the water and dreams. The Rat explains that the River is his whole world — his family, his food, his fun, in every season — and that the bank is so busy with otters, kingfishers and moorhens that he's never lonely.
explain: The Mole points to a dark wood on one side. «That's just the Wild Wood,» says the Rat shortly. Badger lives in the middle of it, and nobody bothers him — but there are weasels, stoats and foxes too, and «you can't really trust them». The Mole, who knows it's bad manners to talk about trouble ahead, changes the subject.
explain: Beyond the Wild Wood lies the Wide World, which the Rat has never visited and never will; he asks the Mole never to mention it again. Then they turn into a quiet backwater by a weir and an old mill, so beautiful that the Mole can only gasp «O my! O my! O my!»
explain: The Mole begs to unpack the basket by himself, gasping at every packet, and then, starving after his early start, he tucks in happily.
fact: The Rat «looped the painter through a ring in his landing-stage» — a painter is the rope at the front of a boat used to tie it up.
fact: The Rat lists the food in one breathless run: «coldtonguecoldhamcoldbeefpickledgherkinssaladfrenchrollscresssandwiches...» — cold chicken, tongue, ham, beef, pickled gherkins, salad, French rolls, cress sandwiches, potted meat, ginger beer, lemonade and soda-water.
fact: The Rat says the other animals call him «a mean beast» who cuts it «very fine» (gives only just enough) — a joke, as the basket is enormous.
fact: The Rat likes the Mole's black fur and calls it his «clothes»: «I'm going to get a black velvet smoking-suit myself some day, as soon as I can afford it.»
fact: «So—this—is—a—River!» says the Mole; «The River,» corrects the Rat.
fact: The Rat lives «By it and with it and on it and in it»: «It's brother and sister to me, and aunts, and company, and food and drink, and (naturally) washing. It's my world, and I don't want any other.»
fact: In February floods the brown water runs by his best bedroom window; in dry times the mud «smells like plum-cake» and he finds things «careless people have dropped out of boats».
fact: The Mole asks «But isn't it a bit dull at times? Just you and the river, and no one else to pass a word with?», and the Rat answers that the bank is too crowded: «Otters, kingfishers, dabchicks, moorhens» all wanting you to do something (a dabchick is a small diving water bird).
fact: About the Wild Wood: «The squirrels are all right. And the rabbits—some of 'em, but rabbits are a mixed lot. And then there's Badger». Badger «lives right in the heart of it» and «Nobody interferes with him. They'd better not».
fact: The others are «Weasels—and stoats—and foxes»: «they break out sometimes ... you can't really trust them». The book's animals are characters in a story; real weasels and foxes are not wicked, they just hunt to eat.
fact: «it is quite against animal-etiquette to dwell on possible trouble ahead» — etiquette = the rules of polite behaviour.
fact: «Beyond the Wild Wood comes the Wide World. And that's something that doesn't matter, either to you or me. I've never been there, and I'm never going, nor you either, if you've got any sense at all. Don't ever refer to it again, please.»
fact: The backwater has a weir (a low wall across a river that water tumbles over) and «a restless dripping mill-wheel» turning beside a «grey-gabled mill-house».
fact: The Mole «had started his spring-cleaning at a very early hour that morning» and had «not paused for bite or sup» (= had eaten and drunk nothing).
fact: Old words: «Hold hard!» = wait!; «luncheon» = lunch; «pitch in» = start eating; «in ecstacies» = wild with joy; «shortly» = curtly, in few words; «a trifle» = a little.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «arvo» (Australia) = afternoon; «bussin'» = delicious; «canny» (North East England) = nice, good; «sus» = suspicious, dodgy; «yinz» (Pittsburgh) = you all; «tamping» (Wales) = furious.
exercise: Where does the Rat fetch the picnic basket from? | His hole in the bank above | A shop in the nearest town | The bottom of the boat | The Mole's house
exercise: What is the basket made of? | Wicker | Shiny tin | Brown leather | Wood painted blue and white like the boat
exercise: What is the first food the Rat names? | Cold chicken | Hot gingerbread | Pickled onions and cheese | Fizzy lemonade
exercise: Why does the Mole cry «O stop, stop»? | The list sounds too wonderful | He hates every single thing in the basket and feels sick | The boat is sinking | He wants to row
exercise: «the other animals are always telling me that I'm a mean beast and cut it very fine!» Why is this funny? | The basket is huge, not mean at all | The Rat really is very mean with food and never shares | The Rat is joking about knives | The Mole is mean
exercise: What does the Rat say he'd like to buy one day? | A black velvet smoking-suit | A bigger boat with sails | A house like Toad Hall | A new basket
exercise: Why does the Rat call the Mole's fur his «clothes»? | It looks like a smart black velvet suit | The Mole is wearing a coat over his fur | The Mole is wearing his whitewash apron | The Rat can't see well
exercise: «So—this—is—a—River!» How does the Rat correct him? | «The River» | «A stream» | «A pond» | «Just a ditch, my young friend»
exercise: Which of these does the Rat NOT call the river? | His enemy | Brother and sister to him | His food and drink | His company
exercise: What happens to the Rat's house in the February floods? | Brown water runs by his best bedroom window | It floats away down the river and he has to build a new one | Nothing at all | He moves to the Wild Wood
exercise: What does the mud smell like in dry times? | Plum-cake | Old dead fish | Summer roses | Rotten eggs
exercise: Is the Rat ever lonely by the river, he says? | No, the bank is too crowded | Yes, very lonely, with only the river for company all day | Only in winter | Only at night
exercise: What lies on one side of the water-meadows, darkly framing them? | The Wild Wood | The Wide World, all blue and dim | Toad Hall and its lawns | A town
exercise: Who lives «right in the heart» of the Wild Wood? | Badger | The Otter | The Rat's aunt | Mr. Toad
exercise: Which animals does the Rat say «you can't really trust»? | Weasels, stoats and foxes | Squirrels and rabbits | Otters and kingfishers | Ducks
exercise: Why does the Mole drop the subject of the weasels? | Talking about trouble ahead is bad manners | He is bored with the Rat's long stories about animals | He falls asleep | The Rat tells him off sharply
exercise: What lies beyond the Wild Wood? | The Wide World | The deep blue sea | The cold North Pole | Toad Hall and its lawns
exercise: Has the Rat been to the Wide World? | No, and he never will | Yes, once, when he was very young and foolish | Yes, every summer on his holidays | He doesn't know
exercise: What do they find at the quiet backwater? | A weir and a mill | A town with shops and a railway station | A waterfall and a cave | Toad's boat-house
exercise: What does the Mole beg to be allowed to do? | Unpack the basket by himself | Row the boat all the way back home | Swim | Climb the mill-wheel
exercise: Why is the Mole so hungry? | He started early and hadn't eaten | The Rat ate all the food first, before the Mole could have any | He never eats breakfast | He ran all the way
exercise: «Now, pitch in, old fellow!» What does «pitch in» mean? | Start eating | Throw something into the water | Put up a tent | Help with the work
test: Zara, proper tamping (furious): «the Rat only packed a sandwich, the stingy beggar». Put it right. | He packed loads | Right: one sandwich and a drink | He packed no food at all | He packed only cake
test: Why does the Rat say the whole food list as one long word? | To show he says it fast, in one excited breath | Because he can't spell any of the words | Because it's a spell | Because he's angry
test: «It's my world, and I don't want any other.» What does this tell us about the Rat? | He loves the river and is happy to stay | He is bored of the river and longs to travel the world | He has no friends | He wants to move
test: The Mole asks if river life is dull, with no one to talk to. Why is the Rat's answer a joke? | He says it's far too crowded | He says he talks to the fish | He agrees it's dull | He says he talks to himself
test: The Rat says the Wild Wood is «just» the Wild Wood, «shortly». What does his manner suggest? | He doesn't like talking about it | He goes there every day for fun | It's a tiny wood | He has never heard of it
test: Ali, bit sus (suspicious) of it: «the Rat said everyone in the Wild Wood is nasty, innit.» Is he right? | No: squirrels are «all right», and Badger | Yes: every single animal there is wicked and dangerous | No: he said they're all lovely | Yes, even Badger
test: «Dear old Badger! Nobody interferes with him. They'd better not.» What does «They'd better not» suggest? | Badger is strong and not to be messed with | Badger is small and weak | Badger is the Rat's cousin and lives with him | Badger is lost
test: What would have happened if the Mole had kept asking about the weasels? | He would have broken animal-etiquette | The Rat would have taken him straight there | Nothing | The weasels would have heard and come
test: Which is TRUE about the Wide World in the Rat's words? | «that's something that doesn't matter» | It is where the Rat grew up | It is a nice day out | It is behind the mill
test: Mo says: «the Mole had a big breakfast before he left home, that's why he wasn't hungry.» Put it right. | He'd had nothing since his early start | Right: he had eggs and bacon | He'd eaten the Rat's lunch on the way | He wasn't hungry
test: Looking back: what was the Mole doing at «a very early hour that morning»? | Spring-cleaning | Rowing a boat | Talking to the rabbits | Packing a basket
test: Looking back: why is everything in the boat so «fascinating» to the Mole? | He had never been in a boat in his life | He used to own a boat just like it | He is a boat-builder | The cushions are his own
test: Looking back: who else did the Mole meet before the Rat, and how did it go? | An old rabbit who wanted sixpence — the Mole knocked him over | A badger who asked his name and invited him home | An otter | Nobody at all
test: Looking back: the Mole says «O my! O my! O my!» at the backwater. How was he feeling about the river when he first saw it, earlier that day? | Bewitched and fascinated | Scared and sad | Bored | Hungry
test: ★★★ How many different drinks are in the Rat's list? | 3: ginger beer, lemonade, soda-water | 2: lemonade and soda-water | 4, counting tea | 1
test: ★★★ Put these in story order: (a) the Rat falls on his back in the boat; (b) the Mole sees the Wild Wood; (c) the Mole knocks over the rabbit; (d) the Mole unpacks the basket. | c, a, b, d | a, c, b, d | c, b, a, d | c, a, d, b
test: ★★★ The Rat says the Wild Wood is all right, and also that you can't trust some animals there. Which is the best way to see it? | He doesn't want to frighten the Mole, so he says only part of it | He is telling a lie to trick the Mole into going there alone | He has never been near it | He thinks it is perfectly safe for everyone
exam: Identify the article which the Rat conveyed from his dwelling to the boat prior to their departure. | A wicker luncheon-basket | A pair of sculls | A black velvet smoking-suit | A map of the Wide World
exam: Ascertain the category of creatures which, according to the Rat, cannot be wholly trusted. | Weasels, stoats and foxes | Squirrels | Otters and kingfishers | Moorhens and ducks of the backwater
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises the Rat's position in respect of the Wide World. | He has never been, never will go, and wishes it not mentioned | He travels there annually | He was born there | He intends to go there soon
exam: It is given that the Mole had neither eaten nor drunk since an early hour. State the term employed in the text for food and drink so forgone. | «bite or sup» | «luncheon» | «provender and potation» | «pitch in»
